## 8. Quick Revision Table

| Concept | Purpose |
|---|---|
| Inheritance | Allows a derived class to acquire members and functionality from a base class. |
| Function Hiding | A derived class declares a function with the same name as a non-virtual base function. |
| Function Overriding | A derived class provides a new implementation of an inherited virtual function. |
| `virtual` | Declares a function as virtual, allowing overriding and runtime polymorphic dispatch. |
| `override` | Verifies that a derived function correctly overrides an inherited virtual function. |
| Upcasting | Allows a base-class pointer/reference to refer to a derived-class object. |
| Object Slicing | Copies the base-class portion of a derived object into a separate base-class object. |
| Static Binding | Function selection based on the declared type when calling a non-virtual function through a pointer/reference. |
| Dynamic Binding | Function selection based on the actual object's type when calling a virtual function through a pointer/reference. |
| Runtime Polymorphism | Allows different derived objects to execute different implementations through a common interface. |
| Compilation | Converts source code into machine code before execution. |
| Runtime | The period when the compiled program is executing. |

---

## 9. The Relationship Between Everything

Think of the concepts as a sequence:

```text
INHERITANCE
    |
    v
Derived classes inherit from a base class.
    |
    v
VIRTUAL FUNCTION
    |
    v
Establishes a function as overridable.
    |
    v
FUNCTION OVERRIDING
    |
    v
Derived classes provide their own implementations.
    |
    v
UPCASTING
    |
    v
Base-class pointers/references can refer to
different derived-class objects.
    |
    v
RUNTIME POLYMORPHISM
    |
    v
The appropriate overridden function is selected
based on the actual object type at runtime.
```

### The Three Most Important Things to Remember

1. `virtual` establishes a polymorphic function in the base class and enables dynamic dispatch.
2. `override` verifies that the derived class correctly overrides that function.
3. Runtime polymorphism allows a base-class pointer/reference to call the appropriate derived-class implementation.

If you understand these three relationships, you've understood the foundation of runtime polymorphism in C++.